---
/**
 * Chico Avenue Portfolio: investor page.
 *
 * Hidden, access-code-gated page for investors in the Chico Avenue Portfolio
 * offering (Bidwell Chico Avenue Portfolio, LLC). Not linked in site nav, noindex.
 * URL: /chico-avenue
 *
 * Three jobs: let an investor pull the underwriting levers and see what happens
 * to their money, read the business plan, and get to the offering documents in
 * the investor portal.
 *
 * Engine: /chico-avenue/model.js (a port of the two per-building pro formas).
 * Access code: compared as a SHA-256 hash (ACCESS_HASH in the inline script).
 * To change the code: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').createHash('sha256').update('newcode').digest('hex'))"
 */
const PORTAL_URL = 'https://bidwellcapital.invportal.com/';
const OFFERING_URL = 'https://bidwellcapital.invportal.com/app/offering-detail/6a838d2dbe73b41cde69e270';
const DECK_URL = '/chico-avenue/Chico-Avenue-Portfolio-Investor-Deck.pdf';
---

<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow, noarchive" />
  <title>Chico Avenue Portfolio | Bidwell Capital</title>
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var stored = null;
      try { stored = localStorage.getItem('theme'); } catch (e) {}
      document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', stored || 'light');
    })();
  </script>
</head>
<body>

<!-- ============================================================ AUTH -->
<div id="auth-screen">
  <div class="auth-wrap">
    <img src="/logo-black.svg" alt="Bidwell Capital" class="auth-logo logo-light" />
    <img src="/logo-white.svg" alt="Bidwell Capital" class="auth-logo logo-dark" />
    <div class="auth-card">
      <span class="section-label">Confidential</span>
      <h1 class="auth-title">Chico Avenue Portfolio</h1>
      <p class="auth-sub">Enter the access code from your invitation.</p>
      <input type="password" id="auth-input" placeholder="Access code" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Access code" />
      <div id="auth-error" class="auth-error" aria-live="polite"></div>
      <button id="auth-btn" class="btn btn-primary">Continue</button>
    </div>
    <p class="auth-foot">For invited investors only. If you were not sent this link by Bidwell Capital, please do not proceed. This page is not an offer to sell securities.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ APP -->
<div id="app" hidden>

  <header class="topbar">
    <div class="wrap topbar-inner">
      <a href="/" class="brand">
        <img src="/logo-black.svg" alt="Bidwell Capital" class="brand-logo logo-light" />
        <img src="/logo-white.svg" alt="Bidwell Capital" class="brand-logo logo-dark" />
      </a>
      <nav class="topnav" aria-label="Sections">
        <a href="#model">Model</a>
        <a href="#plan">Business plan</a>
        <a href="#properties">Properties</a>
        <a href="#terms">Terms</a>
        <a href="#documents">Documents</a>
      </nav>
      <a href={PORTAL_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-outline btn-sm">Investor Portal ↗</a>
    </div>
    <nav class="subnav" aria-label="Sections">
      <a href="#model">Model</a><a href="#plan">Plan</a><a href="#properties">Properties</a><a href="#terms">Terms</a><a href="#documents">Documents</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hero">
    <img src="/chico-avenue/1017-esplanade.jpg" alt="1017 Esplanade, Chico" class="hero-img" />
    <div class="hero-shade"></div>
    <div class="wrap hero-inner">
      <span class="section-label">Confidential · September 2026 · Supplements the PPM, which controls</span>
      <h1>Chico Avenue Portfolio</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">36 apartment units across two properties on the Esplanade in downtown Chico, California. Under contract with an estate at about $111,000 a door, net of credits, to be run the way we run 946 Cherry ten blocks away.</p>
      <div class="hero-stats">
        <div><b>36</b><span>units, two buildings</span></div>
        <div><b>~$111K</b><span>per door, net of credits</span></div>
        <div><b id="hero-irr">12.6%</b><span>projected 10-year annual return (IRR), base case, pre-tax*</span></div>
        <div><b id="hero-mult">2.3x</b><span>projected equity multiple, base case*</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">*Projections, not guarantees. Assumptions and risks are below. The Private Placement Memorandum and Supplement No. 1 control.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a href="#model" class="btn btn-primary">Run the numbers</a>
        <a href="#plan" class="btn btn-outline">Read the plan</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ MODEL -->
  <section id="model" class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="block-head">
        <span class="section-label">The model</span>
        <h2>Our assumptions, with the levers in your hands.</h2>
        <p>Everything below starts at our underwriting. Move any lever and the results update on your investment. The engine reproduces our per-building underwriting sheets at their assumptions, and it is stricter than they are when you move away from them: refinances have to pass a lender's coverage test, loans mature on their own terms, and any operating shortfall shows up as a capital call instead of disappearing.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="model-grid">
        <!-- LEVERS -->
        <aside class="levers" id="levers">
          <div class="lever-group">
            <div class="lever-row-head">
              <span class="lever-title">Scenario</span>
              <button type="button" id="btn-reset" class="link-btn">Reset to underwriting</button>
            </div>
            <div class="seg" id="presets">
              <button type="button" data-preset="down">Stress</button>
              <button type="button" data-preset="base" class="active">Underwriting</button>
              <button type="button" data-preset="up">Upside</button>
              <button type="button" data-preset="custom" class="seg-custom" hidden>Custom</button>
            </div>
            <p class="lever-note">Neither Stress nor Upside is a floor or a ceiling. Any scenario you build is your assumptions, not the Manager's projection. Losses, including total loss, are possible.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="lever-group">
            <span class="lever-title">Portfolio</span>
            <div class="seg" id="selection">
              <button type="button" data-sel="both" class="active">Both</button>
              <button type="button" data-sel="p1017">1017 only</button>
              <button type="button" data-sel="ra">Royal Arms only</button>
            </div>
            <p class="lever-note">Each escrow stands alone. If a property does not close, the money allocated to it is refunded, and its returns leave with it.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="lever-group">
            <label class="lever-title" for="inp-invest">Your investment</label>
            <div class="money-input"><span>$</span><input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="inp-invest" value="100,000" /></div>
            <p class="lever-note" id="invest-note">$100,000 minimum; the Manager may accept less. Everything below is scaled to this amount.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="lever-group">
            <span class="lever-title">Operations</span>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-rentAdj">Stabilized rents vs. our target</span><output id="out-rentAdj" for="inp-rentAdj">0%</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-rentAdj" min="-15" max="10" step="0.5" value="0" aria-labelledby="lbl-rentAdj" />
              <div class="lever-hint" id="hint-rentAdj"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-stab">Years to reach target rents</span><output id="out-stab" for="inp-stab">2 years</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-stab" min="2" max="5" step="1" value="2" aria-labelledby="lbl-stab" />
              <div class="lever-hint">Underwriting steps rents up in year 2 and reaches our targets in year 3. A slower lease-up stretches that ramp.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-rentGrowth">Rent growth per year, after that</span><output id="out-rentGrowth" for="inp-rentGrowth">3.0%</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-rentGrowth" min="0" max="5" step="0.25" value="3" aria-labelledby="lbl-rentGrowth" />
              <div class="lever-hint">Also grows laundry and utility income from year 2.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-vacancy">Vacancy</span><output id="out-vacancy" for="inp-vacancy">4.0%</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-vacancy" min="2" max="10" step="0.5" value="4" aria-labelledby="lbl-vacancy" />
              <div class="lever-hint">Another 3% of rent is lost every year to move-in specials, below-market leases and unpaid rent, regardless of this setting.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-expGrowth">Expense growth per year</span><output id="out-expGrowth" for="inp-expGrowth">2.5%</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-expGrowth" min="1" max="5" step="0.25" value="2.5" aria-labelledby="lbl-expGrowth" />
              <div class="lever-hint">Property taxes grow at the 2% Prop 13 cap regardless.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-rehab">Renovation budget vs. plan</span><output id="out-rehab" for="inp-rehab">On plan</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-rehab" min="0" max="50" step="5" value="0" aria-labelledby="lbl-rehab" />
              <div class="lever-hint">Plan is about $18,750 per unit at 1017 and $15,500 at Royal Arms. An overrun raises the total raise, so a fixed investment buys a smaller share.</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="lever-group">
            <span class="lever-title">Capital markets</span>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-capRate">Cap rate at refinance and sale</span><output id="out-capRate" for="inp-capRate">6.50%</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-capRate" min="5.5" max="8.5" step="0.1" value="6.5" aria-labelledby="lbl-capRate" />
              <div class="lever-hint">Lower cap rate means a higher building value. Underwriting uses 6.5% at both refinance and sale, the same as today.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-rateDelta">Purchase loan rates</span><output id="out-rateDelta" for="inp-rateDelta"></output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-rateDelta" min="-1" max="2" step="0.125" value="0" aria-labelledby="lbl-rateDelta" />
              <div class="lever-hint">1017: Five Star Bank expression of interest at about 6.6%, 12 months interest-only, five-year fixed rate then reset. Royal Arms: 6.5% modeled with five years interest-only; no lender has issued a term sheet.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever lever-toggle">
              <label class="toggle"><input type="checkbox" id="inp-refiOn" checked /><span class="toggle-track"></span><span>Include the refinances</span></label>
              <div class="lever-hint">Royal Arms refinances at the start of year 4 and 1017 at year 5, sized at the loan-to-value below and capped at 1.25x coverage. Off means each loan runs to its own reset or maturity: 1017 resets its rate after year 5, and the Royal Arms loan matures after year 5 and is replaced at the refinance rate.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever lever-toggle" id="lever-refi2">
              <label class="toggle"><input type="checkbox" id="inp-refi2On" checked /><span class="toggle-track"></span><span>Include the second refinances in year 9</span></label>
              <div class="lever-hint">Underwriting refinances both buildings again in year 9. That returns most of the remaining capital before sale but costs fees, so it trims the projected IRR slightly.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever" id="lever-refiRate">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-refiRate">Refinance rate</span><output id="out-refiRate" for="inp-refiRate">6.50%</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-refiRate" min="5" max="9" step="0.125" value="6.5" aria-labelledby="lbl-refiRate" />
            </div>
            <div class="lever" id="lever-refiLTV">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-refiLTV">Refinance loan size, % of value</span><output id="out-refiLTV" for="inp-refiLTV">65%</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-refiLTV" min="50" max="70" step="5" value="65" aria-labelledby="lbl-refiLTV" />
              <div class="lever-hint">A bigger loan returns more capital sooner and leaves less at sale. The coverage test can size the loan below this.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lever">
              <div class="lever-label"><span id="lbl-hold">Sell after</span><output id="out-hold" for="inp-hold">10 years</output></div>
              <input type="range" id="inp-hold" min="3" max="10" step="1" value="10" aria-labelledby="lbl-hold" />
              <div class="lever-hint">Sale at the end of the final year at the cap rate above, less 4% cost of sale. The plan is a 5 to 10 year hold at the Manager's discretion.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <!-- RESULTS -->
        <div class="results">
          <div class="mini-kpis" id="mini-kpis" aria-live="polite"></div>
          <div class="kpis" id="kpis"></div>
          <div class="events" id="events" hidden></div>

          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-head">
              <h3>What comes back on your investment, by year</h3>
              <div class="legend">
                <span><i style="background:var(--c-cash)"></i>Cash flow</span>
                <span><i style="background:var(--c-refi)"></i>Refinance proceeds</span>
                <span><i style="background:var(--c-sale)"></i>Sale proceeds</span>
                <span id="legend-call" hidden><i style="background:var(--c-call)"></i>Capital call</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chart-dist" role="img" aria-label="Annual distributions on your investment"></canvas></div>
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <div class="card-head"><h3>Year by year</h3></div>
            <p class="table-note" id="table-note"></p>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table class="pf" id="table-years"></table>
            </div>
            <p class="fine">Cash yield is your cash flow for the year divided by your original investment. NOI is net operating income (rent less operating costs) for the whole portfolio; coverage is NOI divided by loan payments, and lenders typically require at least 1.25x.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="two-col">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Sources and uses</h3>
              <table class="su" id="table-su"></table>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
              <h3>What Bidwell earns</h3>
              <p class="card-lede">Every dollar below is already deducted from the returns shown. The Manager is Bidwell Capital Fund GP, LLC, an affiliate of Bidwell Capital; property management is paid to the Manager, not to a third party.</p>
              <table class="su" id="table-gp"></table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <h3>How this works, and what is left out</h3>
            <ul class="how">
              <li>Rents step from today's rent roll to an interim level in year 2 (the Royal Arms appraisal's market rents; a conservative step for 1017), then to our target rents in year 3, then grow at the rate you set. Both properties are underwritten at 4% vacancy plus 3% credit loss every year.</li>
              <li>Cash flow splits 80% to investors and 20% to the Manager every quarter. Refinance and sale proceeds return investor capital in full before the 80/20 split.</li>
              <li>Refinances are sized on the prior year's income at the cap rate above, at the lesser of the loan-to-value you set and a 1.25x coverage test, with a 1% refinance and guaranty fee to the Manager and 1% closing costs. A refinance is skipped when its fees exceed the cash it produces.</li>
              <li>Fees included: 2% acquisition fee on the contract price ($84,200), 5.5% of collections for property management, 1% of revenues for asset management, and the 1% refinance and guaranty fee. No disposition fee.</li>
              <li>Not included: interest on the short-term bridge loan from Wesley Hill if subscriptions lag closing (Supplement No. 1, Section 4), the reimbursement of his $250,000 deposit, offering and organizational expenses, any prepayment premium if the 1017 loan is refinanced with a different lender within five years, and your income taxes.</li>
              <li>Operating reserves of $148,851 are raised at closing and held. The model never draws them and never returns them, which is conservative. Any operating shortfall is shown as a capital call funded by investors.</li>
              <li>Distributions are modeled once a year at year end; actual distributions are quarterly. The sale assumes the buyer pays the same cap rate the model uses for refinances.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p class="fine">Projections are illustrative, unaudited, and depend on the assumptions shown. They are not a guarantee of results. Scenarios you build are your assumptions, not the Manager's projections, and the Manager has not reviewed them. The Private Placement Memorandum, as supplemented, controls.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ PLAN -->
  <section id="plan" class="block block-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="block-head">
        <span class="section-label">Business plan</span>
        <h2>Buy below retail, reset operations, turn units, refinance.</h2>
        <p id="plan-lede">Combined, the two properties are underwritten at about $283K of year-1 net operating income (NOI, rent less operating costs). The plan takes that to about $383K by year 3 without betting on the market. Here is how.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step"><b>1</b><h3>Buy below retail</h3><p>Two buildings from an estate, each in its own escrow with a set walk-away price. $229,000 of seller repair credits negotiated against the inspection findings, including the Royal Arms roof.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><b>2</b><h3>Reset operations day one</h3><p>Insurance, landscaping and pest re-bid across the portfolio. Managed by Bidwell directly, with no third-party markup. The Royal Arms on-site manager expense goes away at takeover. Utility billing standardized across all 36 units where leases and California law permit.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><b>3</b><h3>Turn units to market</h3><p>In-house crews renovate at an estimated $15,500 to $18,750 per unit: LVP floors, counters, plumbing and electrical fixtures, cabinet doors. Rents reset at turnover. We prove the ceiling on a test group before spending the full budget.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><b>4</b><h3>Refinance or move up</h3><p>The pro forma refinances Royal Arms in year 4 and 1017 in year 5 at 65% of stabilized value, returning about half of investor capital while both buildings are kept, and models second refinances in year 9 that return most of the rest. The alternative is a 1031 exchange (a tax-deferred swap) into a larger asset.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="plan-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <span class="section-label">Why downtown Chico</span>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li><b>Little new supply.</b> We have not seen new small-multifamily construction downtown in years, and none is proposed near these blocks.</li>
            <li><b>Below replacement cost.</b> About $111,000 per door sits well below what it would cost to build an apartment in Chico today.</li>
            <li><b>Turnover drives rents.</b> Long-held buildings sit below market and rents reset at turnover. Units are largely in original condition.</li>
            <li><b>The spread.</b> Stabilized NOI is underwritten at 8.2% of net price, renovation and fees. Stabilized buildings have been trading at 6.0% to 6.5% cap rates; the Royal Arms appraisal is in the underwriting file. <span id="exit-cap-note"></span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <span class="section-label">Where the value comes from</span>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li><b>Rents to market at turnover.</b> $1,053 to $1,171 in place today against average targets of $1,329 to $1,404 by year 3, about +20% at 1017 and +26% at Royal Arms. Turnover does most of the work before any renovation premium.</li>
            <li><b>Utility recovery.</b> Most leases already pay a $50 utility fee. We standardize billing and redirect the $35 resident-benefit fee that now goes to a screening vendor, where leases and California law permit.</li>
            <li><b>Expense reset.</b> Insurance re-quoted off the seller's surplus-lines pricing, services re-bid, reassessed property taxes already in the model.</li>
            <li><b>Renovation premium.</b> Modeled rents stay below what we believe fully renovated units can achieve.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card card-wide">
          <span class="section-label">Track record: 946 Cherry Street, year one (affiliate-owned, not part of this offering)</span>
          <p>20 studio units bought August 2025 for $1.46M, about ten blocks away. Similar tenant base, same crews. Rents were pushed with almost no renovation. Operations did the work: utility billing from day one, in-house W-2 crews for maintenance and turns, and leases reset to market at every turnover.</p>
          <div class="proof">
            <div><b>99.2%</b><span>trailing-12 occupancy</span></div>
            <div><b>+34%</b><span>full-year NOI vs. our acquisition plan ($130,165 vs. $96,867)</span></div>
            <div><b>+15.7%</b><span>average rent since acquisition</span></div>
            <div><b>$360</b><span>total delinquency across 20 units</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="fine">Unaudited figures from the property's operating records through July 2026, compared with the Manager's own acquisition pro forma, which is available on request. 946 Cherry is owned by an affiliate of the Manager, is a different unit type (studios), and is not owned by the Company. Past performance of other properties does not predict this Company's results.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card card-wide">
          <span class="section-label">Timeline</span>
          <div class="timeline">
            <div><b>Oct 2026 (target)</b><span>Close both escrows, subject to financing and the offering minimum. Management rollover ready for day one.</span></div>
            <div><b>2026</b><span>Reset. Billing, taxes, insurance and services re-bid before takeover.</span></div>
            <div><b>2027 to 2028</b><span>Reposition. Interior turns in phases. A test group proves the ceiling first.</span></div>
            <div><b>2029 to 2030</b><span>Stabilize. Full rent roll at market. Season the income for the refinance.</span></div>
            <div><b>2030+</b><span>Refinance or move up. Cash flow on a reduced basis, or 1031 into a larger building.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ PROPERTIES -->
  <section id="properties" class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="block-head">
        <span class="section-label">The properties</span>
        <h2>Two buildings, one block apart, one operation.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="props" id="props"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ TERMS -->
  <section id="terms" class="block block-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="block-head">
        <span class="section-label">Terms</span>
        <h2>Straight 80/20, quarterly distributions, capital back first.</h2>
        <p>Summary only. The operating agreement, PPM, Supplement No. 1 and subscription documents control.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="terms">
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Split</span><p>80% of distributable cash flow to investors, 20% to the Manager. No preferred return, so the Manager shares from the first dollar of cash flow.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Capital first</span><p>On a sale or refinance, investor capital is returned in full before remaining proceeds are split 80/20.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Fees</span><p>2% acquisition fee on the contract price ($84,200) at closing. 5.5% of collections for property management, paid to the Manager. 1% of revenues for asset management. 1% refinance and guaranty fee on gross refinance proceeds. No disposition fee. All are deducted in the returns shown; see "What Bidwell earns" above for the dollars.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Distributions</span><p>Quarterly, beginning once operations support them. Year one is underwritten at about 3.7% on invested capital while units turn, so this is not a current-income vehicle in its first year.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Offering</span><p>Up to $4,000,000 of Class A Units under Rule 506(b), rolling closes on identical terms. $100,000 minimum; the Manager may accept less. Subscriptions are held at Chase and returned without interest if $600,000 is not raised. Each subscription is allocated 32.46% to 1017 and 67.54% to Royal Arms; if a property does not close, that portion is refunded, not redeployed. The Manager expects to accept about $2.23M to $2.5M. Hold of 5 to 10 years at the Manager's discretion.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Debt</span><p>1017 Esplanade: $975,000 per a Five Star Bank expression of interest, not a commitment: 5-year Treasury plus 2.25%, about 6.6% today, fixed five years then reset, 10-year term, 12 months interest-only then 25-year amortization, recourse with Adam Penn's personal guaranty, 1.25x coverage covenant, prepayment premium in years 1 to 5. Royal Arms: about $1.7M modeled at 6.5% with five years interest-only. No lender has issued a term sheet for Royal Arms.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Structure</span><p>Bidwell Chico Avenue Portfolio, LLC holds 1017 Esplanade directly and Royal Arms through a wholly owned subsidiary so each property carries its own lender. Manager: Bidwell Capital Fund GP, LLC. The Manager will manage the buildings itself and does not hold a California real estate broker license; see the PPM risk factors.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="section-label">Sponsor alignment</span><p>The Manager intends to subscribe for at least $10,000 of Class A Units on the same terms as other investors; the amount is not fixed and may be funded from the acquisition fee. Adam Penn personally guarantees the 1017 loan and is paid the 1% guaranty fee above for doing so. Bidwell is the operator, not just the sponsor. See the PPM for the Manager's fees and conflicts of interest.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="risks">
        <span class="section-label">What can go wrong</span>
        <ul class="bullets">
          <li><b>Execution.</b> The return is manufactured through turns and operations. Slow lease-up or renovation overruns push the timeline right. Use the "years to reach target rents" and renovation levers above to see the effect.</li>
          <li><b>Financing.</b> Neither loan is committed. Worse terms, or no loan for Royal Arms, would lower returns or prevent that acquisition. Both loans carry interest-only periods and then reset or mature in year 5, and the plan depends on refinancing.</li>
          <li><b>Concentration and structure.</b> Royal Arms is roughly two-thirds of the equity: one property, one roof, one reposition plan. Investors' interest in Royal Arms sits behind its lender through a subsidiary.</li>
          <li><b>Rents.</b> Year 1 uses today's rent roll. Years 2 and 3 assume rents step up and then reach our targets as units turn, about +20% at 1017 and +26% at Royal Arms. Chico rents have softened from their 2024 peak; if that continues, those steps take longer or do not happen.</li>
          <li><b>Expenses.</b> Property taxes roughly double on reassessment at purchase and are modeled that way. City sewer rates step up sharply from late 2026, to be recovered through utility billing where leases and California law allow.</li>
          <li><b>Roof and capital items.</b> The Royal Arms roof certification comes from the seller's contractor, is conditioned on minor repairs, and defers rather than eliminates replacement. Renovation costs may exceed budget.</li>
          <li><b>Bridge financing.</b> If subscriptions lag closing, the Company may borrow short term from Wesley Hill at about 1.1% a month, which is not in the model.</li>
          <li><b>Illiquidity.</b> Interests are restricted securities with no redemption right and no public market. Plan on holding five years or more.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="fine">Supplement No. 1 adds eight risk factors specific to the two-property portfolio. Read them.</p>
      </div>
